---
/*
 * The inbound half of the interceptor, which is where the protocol's guarantee
 * actually lives. The ticket-granting ticket opens under the TGS's own long-term
 * key; the session key comes out of the ticket; that session key opens the
 * client's authenticator. So the server proves the client holds a key it never
 * transmitted, and the session key is the accent because it is the piece that was
 * never on the wire.
 *
 * Shared canvas and styling: the .diagram rules in src/styles/global.css.
 */
---

<figure class="diagram">
  <div class="diagram-scroll">
    <svg
      viewBox="-8 -10 596 240"
      role="img"
      aria-labelledby="nested-title nested-desc"
      xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"
    >
      <title id="nested-title">Nested decryption of a ticket-granting ticket</title>
      <desc id="nested-desc">
        The client sends a ticket-granting ticket and an authenticator. The interceptor decrypts the
        ticket with the Ticket Granting Service's long-term key, which yields a session key from inside
        the ticket. That session key then decrypts the authenticator. The session key never crosses the
        network, so opening the authenticator proves the client holds it.
      </desc>

      <g class="edge-label">
        <text x="0" y="0">one interceptor, every KDC endpoint</text>
      </g>

      <!-- What arrives, each half sealed under a different key. -->
      <g class="node">
        <rect x="0" y="20" width="216" height="34" rx="3" />
        <rect x="0" y="76" width="216" height="34" rx="3" />
      </g>
      <g class="field">
        <text x="14" y="42">TGT, sealed</text>
        <text x="14" y="98">authenticator, sealed</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Step one: the TGS opens the ticket with its own long-term key. -->
      <g class="collapse">
        <line x1="228" y1="37" x2="296" y2="37" />
        <polygon points="296,31 310,37 296,43" />
        <text x="262" y="25" text-anchor="middle">TGS key</text>
      </g>

      <g class="node">
        <rect x="322" y="20" width="216" height="34" rx="3" />
      </g>
      <g class="field">
        <text x="336" y="42">ticket contents</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Step two: the session key is lifted out of the opened ticket. -->
      <g class="accent">
        <line x1="430" y1="54" x2="430" y2="70" />
        <polygon points="424,70 430,84 436,70" />
        <rect x="322" y="76" width="216" height="34" rx="3" />
        <text x="336" y="98">session key</text>
      </g>
      <g class="edge-label">
        <text x="452" y="70">never sent</text>
      </g>

      <!-- Step three: that session key opens the authenticator. -->
      <g class="accent">
        <line x1="322" y1="93" x2="242" y2="93" />
        <polygon points="242,87 228,93 242,99" />
      </g>
      <g class="edge-label">
        <text x="275" y="85" text-anchor="middle">opens</text>
      </g>

      <g class="collapse">
        <line x1="108" y1="110" x2="108" y2="146" />
        <polygon points="102,146 108,160 114,146" />
      </g>

      <g class="node">
        <rect x="0" y="166" width="538" height="34" rx="3" />
      </g>
      <g class="field">
        <text x="14" y="188">the client holds a key it never transmitted</text>
      </g>
    </svg>
  </div>

  <figcaption>
    The ticket opens under the TGS key, the session key comes out of the ticket, and that key opens the
    authenticator.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
